## Branching

- Feature branches per unit of work: `feat/<short-name>`, `fix/<short-name>`,
  `chore/<short-name>`, branched from `[integration branch name]`.
- Commit on the branch; open a PR (or equivalent) into `[integration branch name]` — [state your
  CI/gate requirement]. Do not force-push a shared branch.
- **This project's branching/release strategy:** [state which shape was chosen and why — e.g.
  "two branches: `[integration branch name]` is where in-flight work lands and gets exercised
  together, `[release branch name]` only moves when a human deliberately promotes integration
  into it; never commit or merge directly to `[release branch name]`" or "trunk-based: everything
  merges to `[main branch name]` behind feature flags; a human flips the flag to release." See
  [`06-source-control-and-release.md`](../../principles/06-source-control-and-release.md) — this
  standard doesn't mandate one shape, but the project's choice must be recorded here, not left
  implicit.]
- One slice = one branch + one (or a few, logically grouped) commits. Independent slices approved
  for parallel work each get their own branch in their own isolated workspace — see
  [`06-source-control-and-release.md`](../../principles/06-source-control-and-release.md#parallel-work).

## Hygiene

- Stage only intended files; never commit secrets, local environment files, scratch content, or
  build/tooling state that shouldn't be tracked.
- A commit that touches code must not leave the suite red, static analysis failing, or
  formatting dirty.
- Update docs in the same slice's commits (docs travel with the behavior they describe).
